# Secrets and local config — never commit (BD-002)
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
# The worker's dev settings, documented (S-22.16); `.env.worker` itself stays ignored.
!/.env.worker.example
*.pem
*.key
*.token

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# Dependencies / build output
node_modules/
dist/
build/
.turbo/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Test and tooling output
coverage/
.vitest/
/test-results/
/playwright-report/
# One part's blob per machine of a split run, read by the merge (`scripts/verify.mjs`).
/.vitest-reports/
*.lcov

# Local data and logs.
#
# Both patterns are anchored to the repository root on purpose. Git treats a
# pattern with no leading slash as matching at *any* depth, so an unanchored
# `data/` silently swallows `apps/server/src/data/`, and an unanchored `*.log`
# swallows a recorded fixture. A source file that is invisible to `git status`
# still typechecks and still passes every test locally, and the mistake only
# surfaces as a build that cannot compile the tree it just pushed.
# `scripts/check-ignored.mjs` (the `ignored:check` step of `pnpm run -s verify`)
# fails the build if any source path is ignored again.
/data/
/logs/
/*.log

# Agent-local settings.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash to match the convention in `CLAUDE.md`, and written out rather
# than relied upon: git already anchors a pattern that contains a separator anywhere but at its
# end, so these two matched only at the root before the slash was added as well (verified with
# `git check-ignore --no-index`). The slash makes the anchoring readable instead of a rule the
# reader has to know, which is the whole point of the convention.
#
# `/.claude/worktrees/` holds linked git worktrees — whole separate checkouts of this repository.
# `scripts/check-ignored.mjs` does not walk into them, and that is a property of the `.git` entry
# they contain rather than of this pattern; see `isSeparateCheckout` there.
/.claude/settings.local.json
/.claude/worktrees/

# A local plugin's state (WP-270 (c)): its own directory, and the files it keeps beside each skill
# it manages. `scripts/os-artefacts.mjs`'s `LOCAL_TOOL_PATHS` names the same four, so
# `ignored:check` stays green; change both or neither. A skill's `SKILL.md` and `references/` are
# not ignored — whether an agent-written skill is committed is the person's decision.
/.claude/autoharness/
/.claude/skills/*/.sidecar.json
/.claude/skills/*/.sidecar.json.lock
/.claude/skills/*/.ledger.jsonl
